speechtext:
There are two points I should like to raise .in the interest of clarification-I hope it is clarification. I suggest that the word "taxable" be inserted before the word "income" in line 32 of page 8 of the bill. If I understand it aright, the income referred to . would be taxable income, but as it reads now, it refers to the income of the company calculated in accordance with this act. That may have no other meaning than taxable income, but if I am right the insertion of the word "taxable" would make it clear. The next paragraph reads;
(ii) the income of the company in each of the said periods that was not liable to taxation under this act.
It has been suggested to me that that might in certain cases include capital gains, and I cannot believe that that was intended. Therefore I suggest that after the word "Act" in line 40 the following words be added: "other than income arising out of the sale or other disposition of a capital asset." That would make it clear. My suggestion is that although that is not taxable income, in certain circumstances it might be deemed income and therefore the addition of these words could only be in the interest of clarification.

In comparing the economy of New Zealand there are two or three things that many of us have not yet grasped. New Zealand is about the size of Saskatchewan. It is a country of a million and a half people. They lack minerals; they have not a drop of oil; they must import all of their oil products;

National Housing
they import their metals; they have very little soal and very little timber. They live on the export market. When you compare the economy of Canada with that of New Zealand you are comparing two things that are not comparable because we have so much more than they have. We should be able to do so much more than they are doing because their economy is necessarily limited by their resources.
